arXiv:2603.12057v1 [cs.CV] 12 Mar 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform Sampling</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yanghao Wang<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">⋆</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ziqi Jiang<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">⋆</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhen Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Long Chen<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">ywangtg@connect.ust.hk, zhenwang@ust.hk, longchen@ust.hk
<br class="ltx_break"><a href="https://github.com/HKUST-LongGroup/Coarse-guided-Gen" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url">https://github.com/HKUST-LongGroup/Coarse-guided-Gen</a>
<br class="ltx_break"></span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Coarse-guided visual generation, which synthesizes fine visual samples from degraded or low-fidelity coarse references, is essential for various real-world applications. While training-based approaches are effective, they are inherently limited by high training costs and restricted generalization due to paired data collection. Accordingly, recent training-free works propose to leverage pretrained diffusion models and incorporate guidance during the sampling process. However, these training-free methods either require knowing the forward (fine-to-coarse) transformation operator, <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, bicubic downsampling, or are difficult to balance between guidance and synthetic quality.
To address these challenges, we propose a novel guided method by using the <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform, a tool that can constrain stochastic processes (<em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, sampling process) under desired conditions.
Specifically, we modify the transition probability at each sampling timestep by adding to the original differential equation with a drift function <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, which approximately steers the generation toward the ideal fine sample. To address unavoidable approximation errors, we introduce a noise-level-aware schedule that gradually de-weights the <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> term as the error increases, ensuring both guidance adherence and high-quality synthesis.
Extensive experiments across diverse image and video generation tasks demonstrate the effectiveness and generalization of our method.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Guided Visual Generation Diffusion Model Doob’s <math id="m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12057v1/Image1.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/226;" width="452" height="226" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Given a coarse visual sample (left one in each pair) as the guidance, our method generates its corresponding refined result (right one) in a training-free manner.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> and flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> models have demonstrated remarkable visual generation capability. Benefited from large-scale pretraining, high-quality samples can be generated by existing unconditional and text-to-visual (T2V) models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> in the case of without guidance or only with text guidance. However, some real-world applications (<em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, deblurring and super-resolution) always require a coarse visual sample to guide synthesis, which these pretrained unconditional and T2V models cannot handle directly. Thus, recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> aim to solve such a <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">coarse-guided visual generation</span> task (<em id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cf</em>.<span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span>, Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>): given a <em id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coarse visual sample</em> (<em id="S1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text"></span>, a blurred image, or a warped video) as the guidance, the model needs to generate the <em id="S1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">corresponding refined sample</em> (<em id="S1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text"></span>, a clear image, or an undistorted video).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A straightforward approach to coarse-guided visual generation is to train translation networks. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a), previous studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> try to train from scratch or finetune a translation model with paired coarse-fine data. For different types of coarse sample (<em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, low-resolution images, blurred images, and warped videos), additional/existing models must be trained/fine-tuned with the corresponding paired data. Consequently, these training-based approaches suffer from inherent <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high costs</span> (<em id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span>, training and collecting paired data) and <span id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">limited generalization</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequent work resorts to training-free solutions that only modify the sampling process to achieve guided generation.
There are two mainstream training-free strategies:
1) <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Solving Inverse Problem</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>:
As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b), instead of sampling from the marginal distribution, they approximate the posterior probability term and sample directly from the conditional distribution. However, the approximation used in these methods usually relies on a known forward fine-to-coarse operator, <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, the transformation from fine samples to coarse samples must be known, such as bicubic downsampling or a Gaussian mask operator. Thus, this prior (known operator) requirement reduces the robustness of these methods, as the operator may be unknown in many scenarios.
2) <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Start-Guided Synthesis</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>: As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(c), they first get the sampling start by adding noise to the coarse sample. Then they can sample from it with a pretrained diffusion model to get the refined sample. However, the guidance is entirely dependent on setting the noisy coarse sample as the starting point. Adding a large amount of noise results in loss of guidance signals, whereas adding a small amount of noise yields only limited quality improvement.
In that case, this start-dependent guided manner leads to an <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unstable balance</span> between guidance faithfulness and generation quality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12057v1/Image2.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/362;" width="476" height="362" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Existing and our solutions.<span id="S1.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (a) Training translation networks based on paired data, which is costly and non-generalizable to different types of coarse samples. (b) Solving inverse problems based on a known forward operator, making it is not robust. (c) Adding noise to the coarse sample and denoising it, which is difficult to balance the guidance and quality. (d) Our method leverages the <math id="S1.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform to achieve training-free, operator-free, and stable coarse-guided generation.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the above discussions, we observe that current solutions are constrained by training, the requirement for known operators, and unstable balance.
To this end, inspired by Doob’s <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, a tool that can constrain the sampling process with the given guidance, we propose a novel coarse-guided generation method <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weighted <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform Sampling</span>. Intuitively, our method incorporates guidance signals during sampling by modifying the transition probability at each time step and finally generates a guided refined result. This probability modification can be interpreted as introducing additional traction toward the <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ideal underlying refined result</em> on top of the original sampling route.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we achieve traction by adding a new drift adjustment <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is the time-index updating latent and <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal result) to the original predicted score <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{s}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{s}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cf</em>.<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(d)).
By sampling with a new score (<math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{s}_{\theta}+{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{s}_{\theta}+{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>), the result can be guaranteed to be <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not tractable because it depends on knowing the ground truth, <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, ideal result <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we can not directly get <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we found that we can leverage another tractable <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate it, where <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the given coarse sample. Subsequently, we analyze the approximation error and find it to be negatively correlated with the noise level of <math id="S1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. To mitigate the error influence, we use a noise-level-aware schedule to adjust the weight of <math id="S1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> across different time steps. That is, as the approximation error increases, we smoothly decrease the weight of the <math id="S1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> across the whole sampling process. In this way, the generated sample not only adheres to the coarse guidance but also improves in quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Weighted <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform Sampling</em>, a coarse-guided visual generation method based on Doob’s <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform perspective by deriving an approximation <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to replace the untractable <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is training-free, requires no prior knowledge of the forward operator, and is stable.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the approximation error and indicate a negative correlation between the noise level and the approximation error. Then, we design a smooth, noise-level-aware weight schedule for the approximation term to mitigate the influence of approximation error.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments across different image and video generation tasks show our effectiveness and generalization.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The diffusion models aim to capture the target data distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by learning a transport process from a prior distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, a Gaussian distribution) to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. To achieve that, a forward diffusion process from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is first determined and can be described with such a stochastic differential equation (SDE):</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\textbf{w}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">w</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\textbf{w},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> is the time-index, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">f</span> is the drift function, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the diffusion coefficient, and <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">w</span> is a Brown motion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, the marginal distribution of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to this SDE is <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have a reversed SDE to describe the reversed process (from <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) of the forward process:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-g^{2}(t)\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})]\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\overline{\textbf{w}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">w</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-g^{2}(t)\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})]\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\overline{\textbf{w}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\textbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">w</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\textbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the revered Brown motion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">According to the Fokker–Planck equation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> (F-P Equation), we can convert this reversed SDE into an ordinary differential equation (ODE) that has the same marginal distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})]\mathrm{d}t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})]\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We can sample synthetic samples by solving the SDE in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1 Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) or the ODE in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.1 Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). However, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> (also known as the score) is not known. Thus, a parameterized network <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> can be trained to predict it. The optimization target is:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\mathrm{min}}_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{x}_{t},\bm{x}_{0},t}\left[||s_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})||_{2}^{2}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">min</mo><mi>θ</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\mathrm{min}}_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{x}_{t},\bm{x}_{0},t}\left[||s_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})||_{2}^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S2.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tractable</span> since the conditional distribution is defined by the forward SDE in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), and it has closed-form solutions. After training, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used as a score predictor to replace <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for solving Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1 Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) or Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.1 Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Doob’s <math id="S2.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a given SDE like Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), the corresponding process can transport <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to a Gaussian noise. However, in some situations, such as image translation, we may need to establish a mapping relation between <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and a fixed point <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, Doob’s <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> proposes to modify the probability transition of the original SDE by adding an <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2}(t)\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2}(t)\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> term to the original drifting term <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">f</span>:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)+g^{2}(t)\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}}]\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\textbf{w}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">w</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)+g^{2}(t)\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}}]\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\textbf{w},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> function. The new transition can guarantee that the SDE ends at the fixed point <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is designed as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. It can improve the transition probability of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and guarantee <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{T}=\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> by adjusting the drift coefficient at each time step.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Weighted <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform Sampling</h2>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12057v1/Image3.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/335;" width="476" height="335" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of Weighted <math id="S3.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform Sampling<span id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. (a) If we have <math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the generation result will be the ideal sample. (b) We leverage <math id="S3.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="1.580em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate the untractable <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and derive that the error is increasing gradually during the sampling process. (c) To mitigate the error influence, we decrease the approximation weight and finally generate a high-quality refined sample.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Formulation.</span> Assuming that there exists an underlying joint distribution <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{\bm{y}},\bm{y})\sim q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{\bm{y}},\bm{y})\sim q</annotation></semantics></math>, which describes the probability measurement between a coarse visual sample <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding fine sample <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the coarse-guided visual generation task, given a coarse sample as the guidance <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, our objective is to leverage a pretrained diffusion model (<em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a.k.a</em>.<span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, the score predictor) <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{s}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{s}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate its corresponding fine sample.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Guided Generation with <math id="S3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a given coarse sample <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, if we have its corresponding ideal fine sample <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can leverage the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform (<em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cf</em>.<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Doob’s ℎ-Transform ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) to modify the transition kernel of the reversed SDE (Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1 Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)) and guarantee it ends at <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>) by minusing<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Different from the adding in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 Doob’s ℎ-Transform ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), here the minusing is because Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1 Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is a reversed SDE, <em id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="footnote1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}t&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}t&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2}(t){\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2}(t){\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the original drifting term:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{d}\textbf{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-g^{2}(t)\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})-g^{2}(t){\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}})]\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\overline{\textbf{w}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">f</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">w</mtext><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\textbf{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-g^{2}(t)\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})-g^{2}(t){\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}})]\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}\overline{\textbf{w}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, after solving this SDE, the endpoint <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> must be <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> (shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a)). To simplify the solving process, we can further convert Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Guided Generation with ℎ-Transform ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) into its corresponding PF-ODE:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\textbf{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)(\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})+{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}})]\mathrm{d}t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\textbf{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)(\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})+{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}})]\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This ODE has the equivalent marginal distribution as Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Guided Generation with ℎ-Transform ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>)<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            This can be proved by the F-P Equation, and we provide the proof in the Appendix.</span></span></span>. Thus, the solution of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 3.1 Guided Generation with ℎ-Transform ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is also <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, it’s obvious that the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not known since <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is off-course unknown as the ground truth we want to generate. Thus, we can not directly achieve the guided generation using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 3.1 Guided Generation with ℎ-Transform ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Approximation for the Untractable <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">As <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}=\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}=\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is untractable, we manage to convert it into a tractable and appropriate approximation. To this end, we propose to approximate it with the following:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}=\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}=\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}|\bm{x}_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}|\bm{x}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.7" class="ltx_p">We name <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}|\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}|\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is another <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> function (<em id="S3.SS2.p1.7.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cf</em>.<span id="S3.SS2.p1.7.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b)). Then, we use Bayes’ rule to change <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> into:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}|\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}|\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})-\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})-\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.8" class="ltx_p">where the second term <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> can be replaced by the given trained score predictor <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{s}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{s}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Besides, we already known the conditional distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{x}_{t};\alpha_{t}\bm{x}_{0},{\sigma_{t}}^{2}\textbf{I})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">I</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{x}_{t};\alpha_{t}\bm{x}_{0},{\sigma_{t}}^{2}\textbf{I}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.9" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sigma_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sigma_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> (noise level) are determined by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> of Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). When <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is also given as the coarse sample, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> is closed-form as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\bm{x}_{t};\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}},{\sigma_{t}}^{2}\textbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">I</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\bm{x}_{t};\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}},{\sigma_{t}}^{2}\textbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> is tractable:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{\bm{x}_{t}}\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\bm{x}_{t}-\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}})^{\textbf{T}}(\bm{x}_{t}-\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">T</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{\bm{x}_{t}}\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\bm{x}_{t}-\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}})^{\textbf{T}}(\bm{x}_{t}-\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{x}_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{x}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.10" class="ltx_p">After that, we achieve to convert the untractable <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> into the following tractable approximation:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}\approx{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{x}_{t})-\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}\approx{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{x}_{t})-\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Approximation Error Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Our proposed approximation in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 3.2 Approximation for the Untractable 𝒉_{𝒙_0=𝒚} ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) inevitably introduces approximation errors. We can calculate the Euclidean distance between <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the approximation error <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E13X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=||\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})-\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}|\bm{x}_{t})||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=||\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}|\bm{x}_{t})-\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}|\bm{x}_{t})||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E13Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=||\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})-\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y})||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=||\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}})-\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y})||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E13Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=||\frac{\alpha_{t}}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{y})||_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=||\frac{\alpha_{t}}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{y})||_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Taking the variance-preserving (VP) diffusion model as the example (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}^{2}+\sigma_{t}^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}^{2}+\sigma_{t}^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), we can replace <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ 3.3 Approximation Error Analysis ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) and get:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E14X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=||\frac{\sqrt{(1-\sigma_{t}^{2})}}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{y})||_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=||\frac{\sqrt{(1-\sigma_{t}^{2})}}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{y})||_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">We can see that the approximation error <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math> is negatively correlated with the noise level <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile, when <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and when <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Weighted Approximation for Error Restriction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">According to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ 3.2 Approximation for the Untractable 𝒉_{𝒙_0=𝒚} ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), we can directly substitute this approximation into the sampling ODE Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 3.1 Guided Generation with ℎ-Transform ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) to get the guided generation result. However, as we discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Approximation Error Analysis ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, the approximation error will gradually become unbearable during the sampling process (as <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is getting smaller gradually).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we manage to design a weight function for the approximated part
to mitigate the influence of the approximation error. When the approximation error is small, the weight can be close to <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, the weight should be decreased gradually to <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Exactly, we found the noise level <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has a good property that satisfies the weight design requirement. Thus, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c), we can use a <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>-related weight function to adjust the approximation term and get the final sampling ODE:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{d}\textbf{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)(\text{s}_{\theta}+\lambda_{\sigma}*(\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{x}_{t})-\text{s}_{\theta})]\mathrm{d}t," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">f</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\textbf{x}=[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)(\text{s}_{\theta}+\lambda_{\sigma}*(\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{x}_{t})-\text{s}_{\theta})]\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is a function of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S3.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS4.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, a power function) such that: (1) <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is negatively correlated with <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math>. (2) <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\sigma}\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\sigma}\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>. (3) <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\sigma}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\sigma}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, the <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> term can drag the sampling path towards the ideal sample <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#C00000;" mathcolor="#C00000">𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.7539,0,0}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\bm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> term is untractable, so we use an <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate it. When the approximation error is small, the traction towards <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is accurate, so we adopt the approximated <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> term more. Otherwise, traction towards <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is inaccurate, so we adopt the approximated <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒉</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒙</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B050;" mathcolor="#00B050">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.6914,0.3125}\bm{h}_{\bm{x}_{0}=\widetilde{\bm{y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> term a little. Finally, our overall sampling algorithm (take the Euler Solver as an example) is shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.4 Weighted Approximation for Error Restriction ‣ 3 Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Weighted <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform Sampling</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Coarse visual sample <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Pretrained score predictor <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{s}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{s}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, Step number <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, Step size <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, Noise schedules <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, Weight function <math id="alg1.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> Refined synthetic sample <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\textbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">I</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\textbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=M</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{t-\Delta_{t}}=\bm{x}_{t}-[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)(\text{s}_{\theta}+\lambda_{\sigma}*(\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{x}_{t})-\text{s}_{\theta})]\Delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">f</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t-\Delta_{t}}=\bm{x}_{t}-[\textbf{f}(\bm{x}_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}g^{2}(t)(\text{s}_{\theta}+\lambda_{\sigma}*(\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}(\alpha_{t}\widetilde{\bm{y}}-\bm{x}_{t})-\text{s}_{\theta})]\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=t-\Delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=t-\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion Models and Bridges.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models have recently achieved state-of-the-art visual modeling capability. Although DDPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, Score matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, and Optimal-transport Flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> provide different perspectives to understand the diffusion family, their success mainly comes from the progressive transport between a prior distribution and the target distribution. Based on these techniques, various large-scale pre-trained diffusion models, such as Stable Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, Flux <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, and Wan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> have emerged and demonstrated remarkable generative performance. In addition to noise-to-visual generation, some works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite> propose diffusion bridges to achieve the visual-to-visual translations. The most typical paradigm is leveraging Doob’s <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> to link the paired samples and learn a joint distribution to transport between them. Our paper is inspired by the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform technique that modifies the probability transition to achieve the guided visual generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Conditional Visual Generation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared with unconditional generation, the conditional generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> aims to handle various modality conditions (<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, text, action, and visual samples) as guidance to constrain the generation range. The text condition is easy to model due to the large-scale text-visual pairs. To this end, current research around conditional generation mainly indicates that the condition is some types of visual signals (<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, coarse sample, stroke, and pose map). Based on different condition types, the task can be specified into image restoration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, inverse problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and conditional control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, and so on. Meanwhile, the solutions also contain training-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> and inference-stage guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. In our paper, we mainly focus on the conditional generation with coarse samples as guidance and generate corresponding high-quality fine samples in a training-free manner.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Coarse-Image Guided Generation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate our effectiveness, we first evaluated on image restoration tasks, a fundamental challenge in computer vision that aims to recover high-quality original scenes from degraded, noisy, or incomplete coarse samples. Specifically, this task entails recovering a clean image <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> based on a given coarse image <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmark.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following DPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, we use the validation set of FFHQ 256x256 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> to serve as the evaluation dataset. This dataset contains <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> clean images of human faces in <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256*256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256*256</annotation></semantics></math> resolution. For the evaluation metrics, we adopt Frechet Inception Distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FID</span>) and Learned Perceptual Image Patch Similarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LPIPS</span>) to quantify the effectiveness. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FID</span> can indicate the distribution similarity between synthetic images and ground-truth images, while <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LPIPS</span> focuses on image-level similarity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12057v1/Image4.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/241;" width="476" height="241" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative results of coarse-guided image generation<span id="S5.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. Compared with training-free SDEdit, our method shows more faithful synthesis across tasks. For fairness, we take their own commonly-used hyper-parameter for SDEdit (<math id="S5.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=500</annotation></semantics></math>) and ours (<math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=5</annotation></semantics></math>), and all other settings are the same.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Implementations.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the protocol in DPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, we transform all clean images <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> by using a forward operator <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and additive noise <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">n</span> into coarse images <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{y}}=\mathcal{A}(\bm{y})+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{y}}=\mathcal{A}(\bm{y})+n</annotation></semantics></math>. Depending on different forward operators, it can be divided into four image restoration tasks: super-resolution (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SR</span>), inpainting (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Inpaint</span>), motion deblur (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MD</span>), and Gaussian deblur (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GD</span>). These coarse images <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> serve as the guidance in the subsequent generation process. For fair comparison, we follow previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and use the same pre-trained diffusion model from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and maintain other settings the same. During sampling, we set the weight function <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\sigma}=\sigma^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\sigma}=\sigma^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and report the average result on <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{5,6,7\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{5,6,7\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compared our method against: 1) six inverse-problem solutions (<em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cf</em>.<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b)): ADMM-TV, ILVR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, PnP-ADMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, MCG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, DDRM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and DPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. These solutions require additional prior for knowing the forward operator. We report their results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. 2) The represented start-guided solution (<em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cf</em>.<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(c)): SDEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, which does not require knowing the forward operator. It has a key hyperparameter: the starting timestep <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can control the tradeoff between guidance and synthesis quality. For fairness, we report the results averaged on three recommended <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from the original paper, <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}\in\{400,500,600\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>400,500,600</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}\in\{400,500,600\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and keep all other settings the same.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We gave quantitative comparisons in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Results. ‣ 5.1 Coarse-Image Guided Generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We can see that: 1) Compared with operator-required methods, our approach can outperform most of them and perform competitively against their best one, <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, DPS. It’s worth noting that we achieve it without knowing the forward operator, which is a strong prior. 2) Compared with SDEdit, our method can outperform it across six of eight metrics, particularly yielding substantial and consistent improvements in <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LPIPS</span>. This verifies that our Weighted <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-Transform Sampling provides a more robust and principled mechanism for coarse-image guidance, leading to superior structural preservation and fidelity. We also gave qualitative comparisons in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Benchmark. ‣ 5.1 Coarse-Image Guided Generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Coarse-Guided Visual Generation via Weighted ℎ-Transform Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. We can see that our results show a better balance between the guidance adherence and image quality than SDEdit.</p>
</div>
